From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:04:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] output: hand a hole-punched surface to a hardware plane

A client that offloads content to a subsurface stacked below its toplevel --
GTK4 does this for a WebKit web view, GStreamer's waylandsink for video -- has
punched a transparent hole in the parent and handed the compositor an opaque
dmabuf covering most of the screen, which it then repaints on every vsync.

phoc textures that through the GPU every frame, and on a phone that is the
single most expensive thing it does. Measured on a Pixel 2 XL (msm8998, Adreno
540, mainline 7.2): m.youtube.com with the video *paused*, nothing on screen
animating, both browser processes at 0 % of a core -- and the GPU pinned at
710 MHz, its maximum, with the die at 77 C. The client was re-attaching a
1440x2481 buffer 57 times a second with full damage and phoc was recompositing
the screen for each one. Closing the browser dropped the GPU to 257 MHz and the
die to 55 C within seconds. The same clip through waylandsink, which is a single
surface and so reaches the existing direct-scanout path, plays at 257 MHz.

The DPU has eight planes. phoc uses one, because it never touches
wlr_output_layer at all: phoc_output_try_direct_scanout() is all-or-nothing and
bails on n_surfaces > 1, and a browser has four.

So promote the largest hole-punched surface to a layer. The rule is strict,
because getting it wrong means painting over the shell:

  - the surface must be stacked BELOW its view's toplevel, which is the client's
    own statement that it punched a hole for it;
  - it must be fully opaque and backed by a dmabuf, so the plane replaces rather
    than blends;
  - no other surface on the output may have an opaque region overlapping its
    box, in either direction -- with that, stacking order cannot matter, which
    is what lets the decision be made without replicating the render walk;
  - and wlr_output_test_state() has the last word, so the DPU's real limits on
    format, scaling ratio and plane count are read back rather than predicted.

Anything that does not qualify is composited exactly as before, and a failed
test loses the plane for that frame rather than the frame.

The renderer then skips the promoted surface, and its box is subtracted from
the buffer damage: when the plane holds the only thing that changed the render
pass draws nothing at all, which is the point. A change in what the plane holds
damages the output whole, because the swapchain's other buffers still have --
or still lack -- the covered pixels.

wlroots implements output layers on DRM only through its libliftoff interface;
the plain atomic interface has no layer support, so phoc asks for libliftoff
before creating the backend unless the environment says otherwise.

Set PHOC_NO_OUTPUT_LAYERS to fall back to compositing everything.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
